#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} \n&= \\lim_{x\\to3}  \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {3x^2}{1}\\[5pt]\n&=  3(3)^2\\[5pt]\n&= 27\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\n}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ But that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1}\\[5pt]\n&= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\\n&=4.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} .$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f236ca-0647-5109-83ea-2610f579e1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286511} {:answer/id #uuid "8b716572-c2cd-567a-8b2b-5d311f0d6a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286512} {:answer/id #uuid "4d72059e-10df-5094-bd80-107ecbac93b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286513} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b22b3b-3db7-56d9-ae03-146b2ceba316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286514} {:answer/id #uuid "9a96ed51-8269-5255-9554-ed9bf372f0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f236ca-0647-5109-83ea-2610f579e1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286511} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a90530-42e9-5508-bcaa-14eff4ccaf5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d78ff30-662b-5fb9-981a-44d909d0721b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114249" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}+2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]}^{′}}{{[2x+2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x+2})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{2(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4617dc05-b7c3-54a3-8ba8-402846b15bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240} {:answer/id #uuid "fa083fa1-257f-52c1-8bdd-4e16083cfdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296241} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5df6a8-9107-5e7c-a593-62f2641b4f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa7d81-a547-5b0b-86d2-58082d6e4845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296243} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244} :answer-field/id #uuid "11cbad53-4033-52c7-8499-d026641bafc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c54e2735-245d-5798-9c48-ef40ec1547e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31253" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x+1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+1}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a9fa45-1fd9-5ad8-baf0-6c183eceedb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358194} {:answer/id #uuid "cd42b75e-0b19-54aa-8e37-03971691861e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358195} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04da96-5dc5-5fd8-909d-c920648a3dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358196} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca95696-6501-56b4-8be4-3d4850c2c883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358197} {:answer/id #uuid "ed48adbf-80fe-5551-9fba-eac4ec9e48e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd04da96-5dc5-5fd8-909d-c920648a3dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358196} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ebccb2-4065-5774-bee3-5949510d5f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f6b45e4-aad3-52ce-a5c4-dc96f14dd157" :question/math-academy-id "q-31272" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{4(2)}]}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea25ec-db6b-58be-a50e-d5eac4a24611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b65e21-4124-599c-8ef5-38bd7add3afa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e257b16a-a775-5397-9d95-12edd8b09eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336903" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{′}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{′}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266154} :answer-field/id #uuid "445ecb86-4291-52cd-9f90-72e378f6a2ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1391e51-b3fc-5d6d-af4b-284d914341dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336951" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} {:answer/id #uuid "4919d0de-8116-5b72-91dd-335bf99cd522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cacba6-2b91-5aed-b8c1-d516321b9e10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb212d02-e01a-5a05-96a9-bd2df6014b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336971" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cde0666-b51f-57d1-99dc-d828b1e49ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271969} {:answer/id #uuid "4de77844-8c60-5e3d-b5bf-1ac26ae36e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271970} {:answer/id #uuid "715ead71-c8e0-5e51-8f10-78732b40ff03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271971} {:answer/id #uuid "3f21e0ba-aff5-58fc-a166-420c799b3531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271972} {:answer/id #uuid "74f0b0bb-7bf1-5f81-bacb-63cf271a62f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4de77844-8c60-5e3d-b5bf-1ac26ae36e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271970} :answer-field/id #uuid "db465870-fc97-59ba-b3fd-af59bd56c29c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe552b19-ecf8-5441-b376-c5bd68d19d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-3771" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{3x}-1]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{e}^{3x}}{1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd470956-f46a-524f-93f6-7cfea4f61dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} {:answer/id #uuid "cad86e4c-2b0f-506d-8803-4288769136a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275731} {:answer/id #uuid "653ab907-40cd-5543-b0cd-e07538abe574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275732} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0555b1-3de4-5f74-b04d-e9711990e5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275733} {:answer/id #uuid "32d005f8-8c9d-5b1c-a2d5-30ae5e4fe9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd470956-f46a-524f-93f6-7cfea4f61dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b9f4c3-b07d-5a7b-a386-ed4be8f82892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5460231d-5ab3-5dda-afe5-f1c42598ed15" :question/math-academy-id "q-72335" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}}{\\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64cb2dc8-7204-5aca-bc9d-3cd91f16892c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264259} {:answer/id #uuid "58beefc3-031f-5cd5-a1cb-88d181ca8560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ebf43-45cc-5265-af34-b7beb51b50f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264261} {:answer/id #uuid "74263b53-7891-5221-9a88-5cdb3a71114a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264262} {:answer/id #uuid "7d70b60a-66f3-5018-8388-0b5c424b7c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d70b60a-66f3-5018-8388-0b5c424b7c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264263} :answer-field/id #uuid "8317f879-b0d4-5e38-b202-437de1124e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6b4e5b-14b3-521d-a29f-b6cecec742c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72336" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
